.TH TEAPOT 1
.SH NAME
teapot \- animate the Utah teapot with VirGL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B teapot
[
.B -t
]
[
.B -b
.I frames
|
.B -n
.I frames
]
[
.B -o
.I image
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Teapot
renders Martin Newell's Utah teapot in a window using
.IR gpu (3).
It uses indexed triangles, smooth normals, perspective projection,
depth testing and per-pixel diffuse and specular lighting.
The mesh and shaders remain on the GPU; each frame updates only the
transformation matrices before drawing and reading back the image for libdraw.
.PP
The teapot rotates with elapsed time. Space pauses or resumes rotation;
.B q
or delete exits. Resizing the window recreates its colour and depth targets.
The caption shows frame rate; window animation is paced at about 60 fps.
.PP
.B -n
exits after the specified number of window frames and reports average
frame rate and time spent submitting, reading back and presenting frames.
Presentation timings separate pixel loading, drawing and screen flushing.
They exclude asynchronous display work and scanout latency.
.B -b
instead renders that many frames without a window or pacing, at 640 by 480.
Setup and the first frame are excluded from timing.
.PP
.B -o
writes the last frame to
.I image
in Plan 9 image format, without opening a window.
It renders one frame unless combined with
.B -b
or
.BR -t .
.PP
.B -t
checks coverage and lighting, reverses triangle order to test depth
occlusion, verifies rotation changes the image, and recreates the targets.
It requires no window.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/gputest
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gputest (1),
.IR gpu (3),
.IR image (6)
.SH BUGS
Requires the host owner and a VirtIO GPU with VirGL.
Image transfer and window composition use copies through guest memory.
Render targets are limited to 2048 by 2048 pixels.
